## Opt-in real-account smoke

TODO: exact environment variable names, sandbox/test account requirements,
least-privilege credential, write/destructive opt-in, command, coverage and
cleanup. Add the fixed connector-type/package/exact-test/cleanup-mode row to
`scripts/provider-real-smoke-manifest.tsv` — the single list the runner and
`scripts/test-provider-real-smoke-gate.sh` both read; the gate test's hardcoded
Provider count must be bumped in the same change. The only release command is:

```sh
bash scripts/run-provider-real-smoke.sh \
  TODO_connector_type \
  '<FULL_APPROVED_HEAD_SHA>' \
  '/protected/provider-smoke/TODO_connector_type.tsv'
```

The runner must reject a dirty/wrong HEAD, a default no-environment SKIP,
test/package FAIL, and a pre-existing output. Its mode-`0600` receipt binds the
implementation commit and exact harness but only describes `cleanup_mode`; it
never substitutes for separately verified cleanup `PASS`. Never document a
real secret or customer identifier.

## Smoke records

Every row must fill the account/auth class, exact API/server version, and
cleanup status explicitly. Do not hide these fields in `Coverage` or a PR
comment, and do not record credential values or customer identifiers.
Keep real-account rows newest first. A `production_supported` Provider uses
the first real-account row as its current release evidence; that row must have
an ISO execution date, concrete non-pending fields, cleanup `PASS` for any
mutating Tool, and an unambiguous result in this machine-readable form:
`PASS; implementation_commit=<full SHA>; harness=TestTODOProviderRealSmoke`.
Historical `PASS`, `PENDING`, and `FAIL` rows remain in the table but cannot
substitute for that current release row. Before production status, remove the
initial undated placeholder or replace it with dated execution evidence;
production history cannot retain an undated real-account row.

